    "author_association": "COLLABORATOR",
    "body": "I just adapted the `tutorials/02-fused-softmax.py` locally to benchmark the JAX jit.\r\n\r\nHere's the function I used\r\n\r\n```\r\n       @jax.jit\r\n        def jax_softmax(x):\r\n            \"\"\"Compute row-wise softmax of X using native pytorch\r\n\r\n            We subtract the maximum element in order to avoid overflows. Softmax is invariant to\r\n            this shift.\r\n            \"\"\"\r\n            x_max = x.max(axis=1)\r\n            z = x - x_max[:, None]\r\n            numerator = jnp.exp(z)\r\n            denominator = numerator.sum(axis=1)\r\n            ret = numerator / denominator[:, None]\r\n            return ret\r\n\r\n        key = random.PRNGKey(0)\r\n        x_jax = random.normal(key, (M, N))\r\n        ms, min_ms, max_ms = triton.testing.do_bench(lambda: jax_softmax(x_jax)\r\n```\r\n\r\nAnd the performance on a V100\r\n\r\n```\r\nsoftmax-performance:\r\n          N      Triton  Torch (native)  Torch (jit)   Jax (jit)\r\n0     256.0  487.256486      532.812985   195.775959   29.441151\r\n1     384.0  571.534884      591.302256   198.493695   44.171646\r\n2     512.0  637.044973      597.819851   203.686088   63.434726\r\n3     640.0  663.991929      630.760349   210.523614   78.523846\r\n4     768.0  692.891613      659.205363   215.697199   87.908789\r\n..      ...         ...             ...          ...         ...\r\n93  12160.0  791.095313      389.899787   201.323201  246.371072\r\n94  12288.0  791.427854      398.609689   201.481331  253.605926\r\n95  12416.0  789.639361      396.407712   201.017958  251.478219\r\n96  12544.0  789.687469      396.917877   201.027533  254.483520\r\n97  12672.0  789.302171      396.896302   201.145972  252.753811\r\n```\r\n\r\nso I think you should use Triton if performance matters more than stability to you. It's harder to use for sure but you'll be able to get good speed-ups if you do anything beyond simple element-wise operations.",
